      "code": "MKTG 7790",
      "department": "MKTG",
      "title": "Psych of Human-AI Interaction",
      "description": "This course takes a behavioral science perspective on the topic of artificial intelligence (AI). It reviews new behavioral insights to help companies thrive in the dawning age of  intelligent machines. This is a non-technical course. No coding or data science skills are required. The course bridges two perspectives. On one side, we’ll acknowledge the tremendous value that AI can provide to firms and individuals. In many ways, automation defines progress. On the other side, we’ll examine emerging risks in an AI-driven economy. The ultimate goal of the course is to help ensure that the amazing technologies currently being developed bring about positive change.\nThe course focuses on Generative AI but we will also discuss other applications of prediction technology. After an introduction to the current state and diffusion of AI, we will review the cognitive foundations of intelligence to sketch a new division of labor between human experts and AI. We will then discuss the psychology of AI, focusing on both  consumers (e.g., customer experience, conversational commerce, synthetic data) and workers (e.g., psychological threats, coping strategies, and impact on skills). The final\npart of the course will focus on topics of great societal importance: innovation, jobs, and ethics. We end by looking at the future of both carbon- and silicon-based intelligence to speculate on what might be coming next.\nIn addition to its many substantive insights, the course offers moments of reflection to help you understand how technology is changing our lives, and how each of us can help effect positive change in the world. Moreover, every Monday we will start with an analysis of the previous week’s news in AI.\nThe main theoretical lens will be offered by psychology, but we will also examine ideas from economics, management, history, statistics, computer science, art, sociology, and philosophy. While also relevant to other disciplines (e.g., entrepreneurship, operations,\nIT, innovation, or general management), the main application contexts will be focused on marketing.\nNOTICE TO MBA STUDENTS: MBA students must request enrollment for this class through Course Match and should not use the \"Add to Cart\" or \"Request Permission\" functions. ************************************************************* NOTICE TO NON-MBA STUDENTS: If there are any seats left after the MBA students register through Course Match, the instructor may choose to admit a few non-MBA students. Only students who have entered a permission request in Path@Penn will be considered. (DO NOT EMAIL THE INSTRUCTOR). The availability of seats will not be known until early January. If there are seats available at that time the department will email you to see if you are still interested in taking the course. This course typically fills to capacity, and it is highly unlikely that any non-MBA students would be granted permission, so plan accordingly by signing up for a back-up course.",
